{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aah, the sound of shaking animal intestines.. I\nmean, strings which are traditionally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5839,
    "dDurationMs": 3481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made out of cat gut but regardless of\nwhat it's made out of when a string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9320,
    "dDurationMs": 3899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrates it does so with the ends fixed to the\ninstrument. This means that it can only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13219,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrate in certain waves, sin waves. Like a\njump rope with one bump or two bumps or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18300,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three or four or some combination of\nthese bumps. The more bumps the higher"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22260,
    "dDurationMs": 3939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pitch and the faster the string has to\nvibrate. In fact, the frequency of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strings vibration is exactly equal to the\nnumber of bumps times the strings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29890,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental frequency that is, the\nfrequency of vibrations for a single bump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34010,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since most melodious instruments use\neither strings or air vibrating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37940,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pipes which has the same sinusoidal\nbehavior it won't surprise you to hear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41250,
    "dDurationMs": 3989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that musicians have different names for\nthe different ratios between these pitches. In"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45239,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the traditional Western scale, 1 to 2\nbumps is called an octave; 2 to 3 is a perfect fifth;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50270,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 to 4 is a perfect fourth, then a major third, minor third some other things that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55090,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren't on the scale and from 8 to 9 bumps is a\nmajor second or whole step. If you play a few of these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60890,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notes together you get the nice sound of\nperfect harmony. Hence the name for this band of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65170,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pitches, harmonics. In fact a sound that matches one\nof the harmonics of a string can cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70500,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that string to start vibrating on its\nown with their resonant ringing sound. And a bugle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74750,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "playing taps uses only the  notes in a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76900,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "series of harmonics which\nis part of why the melody of taps rings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80780,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so purely and why you can play taps with the\nharmonics of a single guitar string."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84770,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Harmonics can also be used to tune\nstring instruments. For example, on a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89060,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violin, viola or cello, the third harmonic\non one string should be equal to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92970,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second harmonic on the next string up. Bassists and\nguitarists can compare the fourth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97300,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harmonic to the third harmonic on\nthe next string up but then we come to the piano or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102310,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "historically the harpsichord or\nclavichord but either way the problem is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105720,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this: it has too many strings. There's a\nstring for each of the 12 semi tones of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109979,
    "dDurationMs": 3981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Western scale times seven. If you\nwanted to tune these strings using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113960,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harmonics you could for example try\nusing whole steps that is you could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118380,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compare the ninth harmonic on one key to the eighth\nharmonic two keys up which works fine for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123360,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first few keys; but if you do it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124610,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "six times, you'll get to what's supposed\nto be the original note an octave up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128940,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which should have twice the frequency."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131380,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except that our harmonic tuning method\nmultiplied the frequency by a factor of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134710,
    "dDurationMs": 9070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nine eighths each time and 9 over 8 to the 6th is not two, its 2.027286529541 etcetera. If you tried"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143780,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harmonically tuning a piano using major\nthirds instead, you'd multiply the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147450,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency by five fourths three times or\n1.953125, still not two. Using fourths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154810,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'd get 1.973 not two. Fifths\ngives 2.027 again. And don't even try"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161260,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using half steps; you will be off by almost 10\npercent and this is the problem. It's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166450,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematically impossible to tune a\npiano consistently across all keys using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170710,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfect beautiful harmonics, so we don't.\nMost pianos these days use what's called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175510,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equal tempered tuning where the\nfrequency of each key is the 12th root of two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179600,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times the frequency of the key below it.\nThe 12th root of 2 is an irrational number"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184140,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something you never get using simple ratios of harmonic tuning;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187220,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but its\nbenefit is that once you go up 12 keys you end up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190340,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with exactly the 12th root of 2 to the 12th or,\ntwice the frequency. Perfect octave!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195540,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the octave is the only perfect\ninterval on an equally tuned piano. Fifths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200220,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are slightly flat; fourths are slightly sharp;\nmajor thirds are sharp, minor thirds are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204760,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flat and so on. You can hear a kind of \"wawawawawa\" effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208060,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this equal tempered chord; which goes away\nusing harmonic tune. But, if you tuned an instrument"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215420,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using the 12th root of 2 as most pianos, digital tuners and computer instruments are, you can play"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any song, in any key, and they will all be\nequally and just slightly out of tune."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228340,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This Minute Physics video is brought to\nyou in part by audible.com, the leading"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231930,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "provider of audio books across all types\nof literature including fiction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234640,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-fiction and periodicals. If you go to\naudible.com/minute physics you can try audible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238989,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out by downloading a free\naudiobook of your choice. I just read"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242309,
    "dDurationMs": 4691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "'The Name of the Wind' by Patrick Rothfuss.\nIt's a fantasy novel with a very music"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247010,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and scientifically oriented protagonist and I thoroughly enjoyed it. You can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250230,
    "dDurationMs": 4119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "download this audiobook or a free audiobook\nof your choice at audible.com/minutephysics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254349,
    "dDurationMs": 2871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I'd like to thank audible\nfor helping me continue to make these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257220,
    "dDurationMs": 509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "videos"
    } ]
  } ]
}
